<ProjectLayout
  slug="online-calibration"
  pageTitle="Calibrating volatility forecasts with early, one-sided feedback · Akshat Valse"
  description="Calibrating volatility forecasts when a miss becomes certain before the horizon ends: a model-agnostic method that uses early, one-sided feedback."
  authors="Akshat Valse"
  affiliation="Department of Computer Science, Iowa State University · Advisor: Hongyang Gao"
  abstract={ABSTRACT}
  buttons={[{ label: 'Public overview', href: OVERVIEW, icon: 'github' }]}
  info="Ongoing · Aug. 2026 – present · Paper in preparation"
  cover={{
    src: `${ASSETS}/early-miss-schematic.svg`,
    alt: 'Schematic of one forecast horizon: two running sums against the same issued upper bound. One crosses the bound partway through, so its miss is certain from that step on; the other stays below, and its coverage is confirmed only when the horizon ends. Toy simulation, not project data.',
    width: 640,
    height: 340,
  }}
>
  <!-- ============================================================ Problem -->
  <Container first>
    <h1>The problem</h1>
    <p class="text">
      Forecast how turbulent an asset will be over the next twenty-four hours, as its realized
      variance (a running total of squared returns), and commit to an upper bound. The total can
      only grow, so if it passes the bound at hour ten you already know you missed. If it stays
      below, you learn you were right only when the day ends.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Risk forecasts for a cumulative quantity like this are usually scored when the horizon ends.
      A calibrator that waits for the complete outcome receives feedback one full horizon after it
      issues each forecast, and that delay can slow its response to a volatility shift.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      But the target’s increments are non-negative, so the running partial sum is monotone. If it
      crosses the issued upper bound before the horizon ends, the miss is <b>certain</b> immediately,
      while coverage is only confirmed at the end (Figure 1). The feedback is early but one-sided.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="large" tone="gray">
    <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 760 400" role="img"
      aria-label="Diagram. Two running sums over one forecast horizon against the same dashed issued upper bound. One path first exceeds the bound at step tau; a marker reads miss certain here, and a shaded band from tau to H reads known for the rest of the horizon. Its end at H is marked scored here if you wait for the complete outcome. The other path, in gray, stays below the bound and ends at a hollow marker, covered: confirmed only at H. A bracket under the axis from tau to H reads lead: H minus tau steps.">
      <rect class="oc-band" x="397.3" y="48" width="162.7" height="282" />
      <path class="line" d="M72,48 V330 H560" />
      <line class="oc-bound" x1="72" y1="180" x2="560" y2="180" />
      <text x="80" y="170" font-size="13">issued upper bound</text>

      <polyline class="oc-path-b" points={D1_COVERED} />
      <polyline class="oc-path-a" points={D1_MISS_SOLID} />
      <polyline class="oc-path-a oc-faded" points={D1_MISS_AFTER} />

