      <text class="muted-t" x="578" y="89" font-size="12.5">scored here if you wait</text>
      <text class="muted-t" x="578" y="105" font-size="12.5">for the complete outcome</text>

      <circle class="oc-hollow" cx="560" cy="203.7" r="6.5" />
      <text x="578" y="201" font-size="13">covered:</text>
      <text x="578" y="218" font-size="13">confirmed only at <tspan class="oc-m">H</tspan></text>

      <text class="muted-t" x="72" y="350" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5">0</text>
      <text x="397.3" y="351" text-anchor="middle"><tspan class="oc-m">τ</tspan></text>
      <text x="560" y="351" text-anchor="middle"><tspan class="oc-m">H</tspan></text>
      <path class="line" d="M397.3,358 v6 H560 v-6" />
      <text x="478.7" y="384" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13">lead: <tspan class="oc-m">H</tspan> − <tspan class="oc-m">τ</tspan> steps</text>
      <text class="muted-t" x="234" y="384" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5">steps into the forecast horizon</text>
      <text class="muted-t" transform="translate(50 189) rotate(-90)" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5">running sum of the target</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 1.</b> One forecast horizon, two running sums against the same issued upper bound.
      One path first exceeds the bound at step <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} />, so from that step on
      its miss is certain; a scorer that waits for the complete outcome still records it only at
      <Tex src={String.raw`H`} />. The other path, drawn in gray, stays below, and its coverage is
      confirmed only at <Tex src={String.raw`H`} />. Schematic drawn from a seeded toy simulation, not
      project data: 24 steps of squared-Gaussian increments (doubled for the path that crosses), with
      the bound at the upper 0.1 quantile of <Tex src={String.raw`\chi^2_{24}`} />.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- ===================================================== What we are doing -->
  <Container>
    <h1>What we are doing</h1>
    <p class="text">
      We are developing a model-agnostic calibration method for quantile forecasts of a cumulative
      target that uses this early, one-sided feedback instead of waiting for each horizon to end.
      Model-agnostic means it does not depend on which forecaster issues the bounds. Theory and
      experiments are both in progress.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      The question the evaluation asks is whether acting on early misses lets a calibrator respond to
      a volatility shift sooner than delayed-feedback conformal baselines, <em>without</em>
      systematically widening its bounds the rest of the time. The question is open, and nothing on
      this page answers it.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      For that evaluation we have built a leakage-audited data pipeline with a sealed test set, a
      simulation framework with pre-registered hypotheses, and delayed-feedback conformal and classical
      volatility baselines, including a temporal convolutional network. The main simulation study
      and the real-data evaluation are still in progress.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      The rest of this page sets out the observation structure the method starts from: what is known
      about a forecast, and when (Figures 1 and 2), and how early that knowledge arrives in a
      classical toy (Figure 3). The method’s construction, the theory and all results are held back
      pending submission.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- ======================================================= Formalization -->
  <Container>
    <h1>What is known, and when</h1>
    <p class="text">
      The public overview states this structure in words; the notation below is introduced for this
      page. It formalizes what can be observed about a forecast, and when, and says nothing about how
      the project’s calibrator uses it. Let <Tex src={String.raw`x_s\ge 0`} /> be the target’s
      increment at step <Tex src={String.raw`s`} />, and let a forecast issued at time
      <Tex src={String.raw`t`} /> have a horizon of <Tex src={String.raw`H`} /> steps and issued upper
      bound <Tex src={String.raw`\hat u_t`} />. Its target and its running partial sums are
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`Y_t=\sum_{h=1}^{H}x_{t+h},\qquad S_{t,k}=\sum_{h=1}^{k}x_{t+h},\qquad 0=S_{t,0}\le S_{t,1}\le\cdots\le S_{t,H}=Y_t .`} />
    <p class="text">
      A miss here is a strict crossing, <Tex src={String.raw`Y_t>\hat u_t`} />; with continuous
      increments a tie has probability zero. Because the partial sums never decrease, a crossing
      settles the outcome before the horizon ends:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`S_{t,k}>\hat u_t\ \Longrightarrow\ Y_t\ge S_{t,k}>\hat u_t .`} />
    <p class="text">Let <Tex src={String.raw`\tau_t`} /> be the step at which the miss becomes certain:</p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\tau_t=\min\{k\in\{1,\dots,H\}:S_{t,k}>\hat u_t\}\quad(\min\varnothing=\infty),\qquad \{\tau_t\le H\}=\{Y_t>\hat u_t\}.`} />
    <p class="text">
      A miss is known at <Tex src={String.raw`t+\tau_t`} />, which is
      <Tex src={String.raw`H-\tau_t`} /> steps before the horizon ends. Coverage,
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau_t=\infty`} />, is known only at <Tex src={String.raw`t+H`} />. That
      asymmetry is what one-sided means here: crossing the bound settles the outcome at once, while
      staying below it settles the outcome only when the horizon ends (Figure 2).
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="large" tone="gray">
    <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 760 290" role="img"
      aria-label="Diagram. An information timeline for one forecast, with ticks at issued at t, crossing at t plus tau, and horizon ends at t plus H. Lane one, watching the running sum: a filled marker at the crossing reads miss known, if it misses; a hollow marker at the horizon end reads coverage known, if it is covered; a bracket from the crossing to the horizon end reads lead: H minus tau steps. Lane two, waiting for the complete outcome: both markers sit at the horizon end, miss or coverage known, and nothing is learned before the horizon ends.">
      <defs>
        <marker id="oc-d3-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">
          <path class="oc-arrowhead" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>
      <line class="oc-guide" x1="300" y1="40" x2="300" y2="240" />
      <line class="oc-guide" x1="500" y1="40" x2="500" y2="240" />
      <line class="oc-guide" x1="670" y1="40" x2="670" y2="240" />

      <text x="20" y="105" font-size="13.5" font-weight="500">Watching the running sum</text>
      <line class="oc-track" x1="300" y1="100" x2="670" y2="100" />
      <line class="oc-track" x1="300" y1="93" x2="300" y2="107" />
      <circle class="oc-acc" cx="500" cy="100" r="7" />
      <text x="500" y="66" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="500">miss known</text>
      <text class="muted-t" x="500" y="83" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5">if it misses</text>
      <circle class="oc-hollow" cx="670" cy="100" r="7" />
      <text x="670" y="66" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="500">coverage known</text>
      <text class="muted-t" x="670" y="83" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5">if it is covered</text>
      <path class="line" d="M500,115 v6 H670 v-6" />
      <text x="585" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13">lead: <tspan class="oc-m">H</tspan> − <tspan class="oc-m">τ</tspan> steps</text>

      <text x="20" y="195" font-size="13.5" font-weight="500">Waiting for the complete outcome</text>
      <line class="oc-track" x1="300" y1="190" x2="670" y2="190" />
      <line class="oc-track" x1="300" y1="183" x2="300" y2="197" />
      <circle class="oc-acc" cx="658" cy="190" r="7" />
      <circle class="oc-hollow" cx="682" cy="190" r="7" />
      <text x="670" y="168" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="500">miss or coverage known</text>
      <text class="muted-t" x="480" y="213" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5">nothing is learned before the horizon ends</text>

      <line class="line" x1="290" y1="240" x2="730" y2="240" marker-end="url(#oc-d3-arrow)" />
      <line class="line" x1="300" y1="235" x2="300" y2="245" />
      <line class="line" x1="500" y1="235" x2="500" y2="245" />
      <line class="line" x1="670" y1="235" x2="670" y2="245" />
      <text x="300" y="264" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5">issued at <tspan class="oc-m">t</tspan></text>
      <text x="500" y="264" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5">crossing at <tspan class="oc-m">t</tspan> + <tspan class="oc-m">τ</tspan></text>
      <text x="670" y="264" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5">horizon ends at <tspan class="oc-m">t</tspan> + <tspan class="oc-m">H</tspan></text>
    </svg>
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 2.</b> When the outcome of one forecast becomes known. Watching the running sum, a miss
      is known at the crossing <Tex src={String.raw`t+\tau`} />, a lead of
      <Tex src={String.raw`H-\tau`} /> steps, and coverage only when the horizon ends. Waiting for
      the complete outcome, both are known only at <Tex src={String.raw`t+H`} />. The diagram shows
      the observation structure only; it says nothing about how a calibrator uses it.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- ======================================================= Toy and widget -->
  <Container>
    <h1>How early is early: a classical toy</h1>
    <p class="text">
      Figure 1 shows that a miss can become certain early; how early depends on the increments. A
      simple model with an exact answer gives a textbook baseline for intuition: a classical
      first-passage calculation, not the project’s method, model or data. Take one horizon of i.i.d. increments <Tex src={String.raw`x_h=c\,\xi_h`} />,
      where <Tex src={String.raw`\xi_h\sim\chi^2_1`} /> is a squared standard normal, the classical
      toy for squared returns at constant volatility, or <Tex src={String.raw`\xi_h\sim\mathrm{Exp}(1)`} />.
      The bound <Tex src={String.raw`u_\alpha`} /> solves
      <Tex src={String.raw`\Pr\big(\sum_{h\le H}\xi_h>u_\alpha\big)=\alpha`} />, so it is exact in the
      calm regime <Tex src={String.raw`c=1`} />, and <Tex src={String.raw`c>1`} /> is a volatility jump
      the bound has not seen. Nothing is recalibrated: every bound stays fixed.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="extra-large" tone="gray">
    <CalibEarlyMiss />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 3.</b> Classical baseline, not project data or method. Left: one seeded run of the running sum
      against a bound fixed at the calm-regime quantile; the filled marker is the step
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> at which its miss becomes certain (a run that stays below the
      bound ends in a hollow marker instead). Right:
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> over <Tex src={String.raw`N`} /> seeded runs against its exact law,
      with Monte Carlo readouts at ± one standard error beside the exact values. No calibration is
      applied; bounds are fixed. Whether early misses make recovery faster is the project’s open
      question.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <h2>Exact reference</h2>
    <p class="text">
      Drop the index <Tex src={String.raw`t`} /> and let <Tex src={String.raw`\bar F_k`} /> be the
      survival function of <Tex src={String.raw`\xi_1+\cdots+\xi_k`} />. Monotonicity turns the
      stopping time into a marginal probability:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\Pr(\tau\le k)=\Pr(S_k>u_\alpha)=\bar F_k(u_\alpha/c),\qquad k=1,\dots,H.`} />
    <p class="text">
      Both families have elementary survival functions. For <Tex src={String.raw`\mathrm{Exp}(1)`} />
      increments it is the Poisson identity, since <Tex src={String.raw`\xi_1+\cdots+\xi_k>x`} />
      exactly when a unit-rate Poisson process has had fewer than <Tex src={String.raw`k`} /> arrivals
      by time <Tex src={String.raw`x`} />; for
      <Tex src={String.raw`\chi^2_1`} /> increments it depends on the parity of
      <Tex src={String.raw`k`} />, with <Tex src={String.raw`\bar\Phi`} /> and
      <Tex src={String.raw`\varphi`} /> the standard normal upper tail and density:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathrm{Exp}(1):\quad \bar F_k(x)=e^{-x}\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\frac{x^j}{j!},`} />
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\chi^2_1:\quad \bar F_{2m}(x)=e^{-x/2}\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\frac{(x/2)^j}{j!},\qquad \bar F_{2m+1}(x)=2\bar\Phi(\sqrt x)+2\varphi(\sqrt x)\sum_{j=1}^{m}\frac{x^{j-1/2}}{(2j-1)!!}.`} />
    <p class="text">
      The quantities the widget reports follow directly:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\Pr(\text{miss})=\bar F_H(u_\alpha/c),\qquad \Pr(\tau<H\mid\text{miss})=\frac{\bar F_{H-1}(u_\alpha/c)}{\bar F_H(u_\alpha/c)},`} />
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathbb E[H-\tau\mid\text{miss}]=\frac{1}{\bar F_H(u_\alpha/c)}\sum_{k=1}^{H-1}\bar F_k(u_\alpha/c),`} />
    <p class="text">the last from the identity</p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`(H-\tau)\,\mathbf 1\{\tau\le H\}=\sum_{k=1}^{H-1}\mathbf 1\{\tau\le k\},`} />
    <p class="text">
      which holds because both sides equal <Tex src={String.raw`H-j`} /> when
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau=j\le H`} /> and vanish when <Tex src={String.raw`\tau=\infty`} />.
      <Tex src={String.raw`\Pr(\tau<H\mid\text{miss})`} /> is the share of misses that become certain
      strictly before the horizon ends; a crossing at the last step, <Tex src={String.raw`\tau=H`} />,
      gives no lead.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      The bound <Tex src={String.raw`u_\alpha`} /> itself comes from bisection on the closed form.
      These closed forms were cross-checked against R’s <code>pchisq</code> and <code>pgamma</code>,
      with a worst relative error of 1.7 × 10<sup>−14</sup>. The widget computes every exact value
      from them; at each setting in Table 1 its exact readouts match the tabulated values, rounded
      to the digits the widget displays.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
